-- allium: 3
-- agent-participation.allium

-- Scope: How an agent participates in one Vera graph, and what the graph keeps
--   saying about which hand wrote each passage
-- Includes: Credentials that name an agent and can be withdrawn, the boundary
--   that derives a submitter's identity from its credential rather than from its
--   claim, and the authorship every block carries so that generated text is never
--   mistaken for written text
-- Excludes:
--   - The wire protocol and its headers (core.allium excludes network protocol)
--   - The model behind the agent, its prompt and its reasoning
--   - Human authentication, which does not exist yet (see identity-access.allium)
--   - Where spoken content came from, which is a different question entirely
--     (see voice-capture.allium)
-- Dependencies: Participant, Membership, Block, Operation and ContributionChannel
--   are governed by core.allium; membership administration by identity-access.allium

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    instance: VeraInstance
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The whole point, stated once.
--
-- An agent in Vera is not a lesser participant. It writes, edits, files and
-- discards through exactly the contracts a person uses, because a second, weaker
-- path into the graph would be a second thing to keep honest.
--
-- What protects the corpus is therefore not a shorter leash. It is that the graph
-- can always answer, of any passage, whose hand produced these words. Herbert
-- writes and the librarian writes; the corpus stays trustworthy because the two are
-- distinguishable at every block, not because one of them was fenced in.
contract AttributedGeneration {
    hand: (block: core/Block) -> Authorship

    @invariant IdentityComesFromTheCredential
        -- Who submitted an operation is whoever the credential names. A
        -- submission that asserts some other participant is refused, not
        -- honoured: an identity a caller can choose is not an identity.

    @invariant ChannelFollowsParticipantKind
        -- An agent's operations carry agent_generation, and no agent can submit
        -- through a human channel. Provenance that the writer could select would
        -- record intention rather than fact.

    @invariant GeneratedContentIsAlwaysDistinguishable
        -- Every block names the participant whose hand produced the text it
        -- currently holds. Reading the graph never requires guessing whether a
        -- passage was written or generated, and never requires walking the log
        -- to find out.

    @invariant AuthorshipFollowsTheLastWord
        -- Rewriting a block transfers its authorship to whoever rewrote it. A
        -- paragraph Herbert wrote and the librarian rewrote reads as the librarian's,
        -- because those are the librarian's words now. Authorship describes the text that is
        -- there, not the text that used to be.

    @invariant OriginAndHandAreDifferentQuestions
        -- A block may name a recording as its origin and an agent as its hand at
        -- the same time. Where words came from and who last wrote them are
        -- separate claims, and neither one implies the other.
}

-- A credential is the only thing that makes an agent an agent rather than a
-- caller. It names one participant, carries what it is allowed to do, and can be
-- taken back without touching anything it already did.
contract AgentCredentialing {
    issue: (owner: core/Participant, agent: core/Participant, scopes: Set<Scope>) -> AccessToken
    revoke: (owner: core/Participant, token: AccessToken) -> AccessToken

    @invariant SovereignOwnerCredentials
        -- Only the instance's founding human owner issues or withdraws a
        -- credential. An agent that could credential another agent would make
        -- the owner's consent transitive, and therefore meaningless.

    @invariant TheSecretIsNeverStored
        -- Vera keeps what proves a secret, never the secret. A credential is
        -- readable once, at the moment it is issued, and cannot be recovered
        -- afterwards from anything Vera holds.

    @invariant RevocationTakesEffectAtOnce
        -- The first submission after a withdrawal is refused. There is no window
        -- in which a withdrawn credential still writes.

    @invariant WithdrawalLeavesTheWorkStanding
        -- Withdrawing a credential changes nothing that was already applied. The
        -- operations stay in the log, attributed as they always were. Revoking
        -- access is not retracting history.

    @invariant ScopeIsCheckedPerChange
        -- What a credential may do is decided against each change it submits,
        -- not once at the door. A credential that may write does not thereby
        -- discard.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a credential is allowed to do. Deliberately coarse: a vocabulary fine
-- enough to describe every change kind would be the change vocabulary again,
-- and nobody would be able to read a token and say what it permits.
--
-- Discarding is separate from writing because it is the one act the graph cannot
-- show you afterwards. Everything else leaves the previous state legible in the
-- log; a removal leaves an absence.
enum Scope { read | write | discard }

enum CredentialStatus { active | revoked }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity VeraInstance {
    graph: core/Graph
    owner: core/Participant
    credentials: AccessToken with graph = this.graph
}

entity AccessToken {
    graph: core/Graph
    -- Whom this credential speaks as. Every operation it submits is attributed
    -- here, whatever the submission claims.
    participant: core/Participant

    -- What proves the secret without being the secret.
    secret_digest: String
    scopes: Set<Scope>

    status: CredentialStatus

    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
    }

    label: String
    issued_by: core/Participant
    issued_at: Timestamp
    expires_at: Timestamp?
    revoked_at: Timestamp?
    last_used_at: Timestamp?
}

-- Whose hand produced the text a block currently holds.
--
-- It is a separate thing from the block, for the same reason SpokenOrigin is:
-- one describes the words, the other describes where they came from, and they
-- change on different occasions. It is also separate from SpokenOrigin, because
-- a block can have both and they can name different participants.
entity Authorship {
    block: core/Block
    participant: core/Participant
    channel: core/ContributionChannel
    written_at: Timestamp
}

-- Un permiso para hacer una vez un acto que está reservado a una persona.
--
-- Hay actos que Vera reserva a una mano humana: fusionar dos palabras del
-- vocabulario, reescribir seiscientas citas, publicar una página. La razón nunca
-- fue que una máquina no sepa hacerlos, sino que nadie debería descubrir que se
-- hicieron. Y esa razón se satisface igual si la persona lo decidió y otro lo
-- ejecutó, siempre que la decisión sea anterior, concreta y esté escrita.
--
-- Por eso es de un solo uso y nombra su objeto. Un permiso que valiera para «las
-- fusiones» sería un cambio de régimen disfrazado de conveniencia: quien lo
-- concede está diciendo que sí a un acto que ya ha visto, no abriendo una
-- categoría. Gastarlo al usarlo es lo que impide que el sí de hoy autorice el
-- de mañana.
entity Delegation {
    graph: core/Graph
    -- Quién autoriza. Siempre una persona; es lo único que aquí no se delega.
    granted_by: core/Participant
    granted_to: core/Participant
    -- Qué acto queda abierto, por el nombre de la regla que lo reserva.
    act: String
    -- Sobre qué cosa concreta. Es lo que impide que el permiso se estire.
    subject: String
    -- Por qué. Escrito por quien concede, y legible después por cualquiera que
    -- se pregunte cómo llegó un agente a hacer esto.
    reason: String
    granted_at: Timestamp
    spent: Boolean

    transitions spent {
        false -> true
        terminal: true
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Autorizar un acto reservado, uno, sobre una cosa.
rule OwnerDelegatesAReservedAct {
    when: DelegateAct(owner, graph, agent, act, subject, reason)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph, participant: agent}

    requires: owner.kind = human
    requires: agent.kind = agent
    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    requires: reason != ""

    ensures: Delegation.created(
        graph: graph,
        granted_by: owner,
        granted_to: agent,
        act: act,
        subject: subject,
        reason: reason,
        granted_at: now,
        spent: false
    )

    @invariant ADelegationNamesOneActAndOneThing
        -- No delegation covers a class of acts or a class of subjects. There is
        -- no way to authorise «merges» or «this vocabulary»: only this merge, of
        -- this word, once. What cannot be expressed cannot be granted by
        -- accident.
}

rule ADelegationIsSpentWhenItIsUsed {
    when: DelegatedActPerformed(delegation)

    requires: not delegation.spent

    ensures: delegation.spent = true

    @guidance
        -- Gastarla es lo que hace que autorizar un acto no sea autorizar una
        -- costumbre. Una delegación gastada se conserva: es el registro de cómo
        -- un agente llegó a hacer algo que por sí solo no podía.
}

rule OwnerIssuesCredential {
    when: IssueCredential(owner, agent, scopes, label, digest)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph: instance.graph, participant: agent}

    requires: instance.owner = owner
    requires: owner.kind = human
    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    requires: scopes != {}

    ensures: AccessToken.created(
        graph: instance.graph,
        participant: agent,
        secret_digest: digest,
        scopes: scopes,
        status: active,
        label: label,
        issued_by: owner,
        issued_at: now,
        expires_at: null,
        revoked_at: null,
        last_used_at: null
    )
}

rule OwnerRevokesCredential {
    when: RevokeCredential(owner, token)

    requires: instance.owner = owner
    requires: token.status = active

    ensures:
        token.status = revoked
        token.revoked_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Nothing here touches the operations the credential submitted. That is
        -- @invariant WithdrawalLeavesTheWorkStanding: the log is a record of what
        -- happened, and what happened does not stop having happened.
}

rule OwnerRenamesCredential {
    when: RenameCredential(owner, token, label)

    requires: instance.owner = owner
    requires: label != ""

    ensures: token.label = label

    @guidance
        -- El alias sirve para reconocer una llave en la administración. Cambiarlo
        -- no cambia a quién representa, qué permite ni el secreto que la prueba.
}

-- The rule that makes @invariant IdentityComesFromTheCredential operational.
--
-- The submitter says who it is and presents its secret, and both have to agree
-- with the credential: the claim is checked, never trusted. What lands in the
-- operation is the credential's participant, so a caller that names somebody
-- else does not write as them — it is refused.
rule CredentialSubmitsChange {
    when: SubmitAsAgent(agent, token, secret, change, origin_id)

    let membership = core/Membership{graph: token.graph, participant: token.participant}

    requires: digest_of(secret) = token.secret_digest
    requires: token.participant = agent
    requires: token.status = active
    requires: token.expires_at = null or token.expires_at > now
    requires: exists membership
    requires: membership.status = active
    requires: write in token.scopes
    requires: discard in token.scopes or not discards(change)

    ensures:
        core/Operation.created(
            graph: token.graph,
            origin_id: origin_id,
            participant: token.participant,
            change: change,
            channel: agent_generation
        )
        token.last_used_at = now
}

-- Authorship hangs off the one door every change already goes through, so there
-- is no way to write a block that bypasses the record of who wrote it. These
-- three rules read the change kind rather than listening for three separate
-- events, because core has one submission event and @guarantee EqualMutationPath
-- is the reason it has only one.

-- @invariant GeneratedContentIsAlwaysDistinguishable made operational: a block
-- cannot come into existence without its authorship, because the submission that
-- creates it records the hand in the same breath.
rule WritingRecordsItsHand {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    requires: change.kind = create_block

    ensures: Authorship.created(
        block: change.block,
        participant: participant,
        channel: channel,
        written_at: now
    )
}

-- @invariant AuthorshipFollowsTheLastWord made operational.
rule RewritingTransfersTheHand {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    let previous = Authorship{block: change.block}

    requires: change.kind = edit_block
    requires: exists previous

    ensures:
        previous.participant = participant
        previous.channel = channel
        previous.written_at = now
}

-- Filing is not writing. A librarian who moves a block from one page to another
-- has not written a word of it, and the block must not start claiming otherwise.
-- Stated as a rule rather than left to silence, because "the hand does not change
-- here" is exactly the kind of thing that gets implemented wrong by omission.
rule MovingLeavesTheHandAlone {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    let authorship = Authorship{block: change.block}

    requires: change.kind = move_block
    requires: exists authorship

    ensures: authorship.written_at = authorship.written_at
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant EveryBlockNamesItsHand {
    for block in core/Blocks:
        exists Authorship{block: block}
}

invariant AgentWritingIsAlwaysMarked {
    for authorship in Authorships:
        authorship.participant.kind = agent
            implies authorship.channel = agent_generation
}

invariant AgentOperationsCarryTheGeneratedChannel {
    for operation in core/Operations:
        operation.participant.kind = agent
            implies operation.channel = agent_generation
}

invariant HumanOperationsNeverClaimGeneration {
    for operation in core/Operations:
        operation.channel = agent_generation
            implies operation.participant.kind = agent
}

invariant RevokedCredentialsAreInert {
    for token in AccessTokens:
        token.status = revoked implies token.revoked_at != null
}

invariant CredentialsBelongToTheInstanceGraph {
    for token in AccessTokens:
        token.graph = instance.graph
}

invariant CredentialsAreIssuedByTheOwner {
    for token in AccessTokens:
        token.issued_by = instance.owner
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor SovereignOwner {
    within: VeraInstance
    identified_by: core/Participant where this = within.owner and this.kind = human
}

actor AgentParticipant {
    within: AccessToken
    identified_by: core/Participant where this = within.participant and this.kind = agent
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface CredentialAdministration {
    facing owner: SovereignOwner
    context instance: VeraInstance

    exposes:
        instance.credentials
        AccessToken.label
        AccessToken.participant
        AccessToken.scopes
        AccessToken.status
        AccessToken.issued_at
        AccessToken.last_used_at

    provides:
        IssueCredential(owner, agent, scopes, label, digest)
        RenameCredential(owner, token, label)
        RevokeCredential(owner, token)
        DelegateAct(owner, instance.graph, agent, act, subject, reason)

    contracts:
        fulfils AgentCredentialing

    @guarantee TheSecretIsShownOnce
        -- Issuing returns the credential in the clear exactly once. Every later
        -- reading shows what it is for, who holds it and when it was last used,
        -- and never what it is.

    @guarantee WhatAnAgentDidIsReviewable
        -- The owner can read every operation a credential submitted, in order,
        -- without leaving Vera. A credential whose work could not be reviewed
        -- would be a credential granted blind.

    @guarantee ExecutionIsDelegableAndJudgementIsNot
        -- An agent may perform an act reserved to a human when a human has
        -- authorised that act, on that subject, once, in writing, beforehand.
        -- What the reservation ever protected was that nobody should discover
        -- such an act had happened, and that is satisfied by a decision made in
        -- advance and recorded — not by whose process ran it.
        --
        -- Two acts stay reserved outright, and the line between them and the
        -- rest is what they destroy. Deleting a property the corpus carried
        -- cannot be walked back by reading a record, and overruling a decision
        -- already taken is the mechanism by which a human keeps final authority
        -- — delegating that one would be delegating the ability to stop
        -- delegating.

    @guarantee ADelegationIsReadableAfterTheFact
        -- Every delegation keeps who granted it, to whom, for what, over what,
        -- why and when — after being spent. «How did an agent come to merge
        -- these two words» is answerable from the graph a year later, and it is
        -- the question that decides whether delegation was safe.
}

surface AgentGraphParticipation {
    facing agent: AgentParticipant
    context token: AccessToken

    exposes:
        token.scopes

    provides:
        SubmitAsAgent(agent, token, secret, change, origin_id)

    contracts:
        fulfils AttributedGeneration

    @guarantee TheSameDoorAsEveryoneElse
        -- An agent's change is applied by the same rules, in the same order, with
        -- the same refusals as a person's. There is no editorial back door.

    @guarantee RefusalsSayWhy
        -- A refused submission comes back with the reason it was refused —
        -- expired credential, missing scope, or a rule of the domain. An agent
        -- that cannot tell these apart cannot correct itself.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should a credential carry a rate or a budget, so that a runaway agent is slowed by the graph rather than by noticing it?"
-- Contestada: sí, acto por acto y cosa por cosa, con la autorización escrita
-- antes y gastada al usarse. Ver entity Delegation y @guarantee
-- ExecutionIsDelegableAndJudgementIsNot. Quedan fuera los actos que destruyen —
-- borrar una propiedad que el corpus traía— y el que un humano usa para
-- rectificar, porque delegarlo sería delegar la capacidad de dejar de delegar.
open question "¿Debe una delegación caducar si nadie la gasta, dado que un permiso concedido hace seis meses sobre un vocabulario que ya cambió autoriza un acto que su autor ya no reconocería?"
open question "Does authorship need to survive a split and a merge the way SpokenOrigin does, and if two blocks with different hands merge, whose hand does the result carry?"
open question "Should the interface show authorship on every block, or only where it differs from the reader's own, so that a corpus that is mostly Herbert's does not become a wall of attributions?"
open question "When an agent's membership is suspended, are its credentials revoked with it, or do they survive a suspension that might be lifted?"
